# The cartridge is the user's own and is never committed.
roms/
*.nes
*.unif
*.unl

# BizHawk itself: 150 MB of emulator, never ours to commit.
#
# This project's own copy is NOT in this repository -- it is a sibling of it, at
# ~/code/games/magician-nes-bizhawk/BizHawk-2.11.1-linux-x64, deliberately so,
# because sharing one install with the aibeatszelda checkout cost that project a
# 136,526-frame verified replay (journal/14). Two checkouts sharing one emulator
# share config.ini and NES/SaveRAM/ with no lock on either.
#
# So this entry is defence in depth, not the mechanism: it stops a 150 MB
# emulator being committed here by someone who extracts a tarball into the
# working tree instead of the sibling directory. `tools/bizhawk/bizpath.sh` is
# the only thing that decides where the emulator is, and `make check-isolation`
# is what proves the sibling is untouched.
/BizHawk-*/

# Generated from the submodule's binary PDS containers by tools/pds_extract.py
pds-text/

# Assembler / build output
asm/out/
*.lst
*.sym
*.bin

# Rust
/target
**/*.rs.bk

# Captured reference frames and scratch
shots/
out/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# BizHawk capture output: screenshots contain cartridge-derived imagery and the
# memory dumps are large and reproducible. Never committed.
tools/bizhawk/out/

# Play-harness output: every BizHawk session log, the recorded input logs, and
# the checkpoints. The checkpoints hold framebuffer PNGs and full dumps of all
# nine memory domains, which is cartridge-derived imagery and cartridge-derived
# data -- exactly what `shots/` and `tools/bizhawk/out/` are already excluded for.
# The input logs are plain button sequences, but they are large and they are
# evidence for a particular run, so they stay out of history too.
logs/
